WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Crew Dragon Grace docked with the ISS after 7 hours and 55 minutes, the fastest US crewed flight to the station. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 145. Starship, static firework, orbital refueling questions, and a falcon stand-down come next. Then the counterpoint and the engineering deep dive. Crew Dragon Grace left the pad at Cape Canaveral at 1110 AM Eastern on October 1st, according to Shattered. The capsule reached Harmony's forward port at 7.05 PM Eastern.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.900
NASA called it the shortest launch to docking interval any U.S. spacecraft has

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:40.420
posted under the previous U.S. crewed mark of 14 hours and 43 minutes on crew 11.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:47.400
Juliana Shimon, SpaceX's program director, credited an opportune station geometry, not

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:53.180
a change in how the vehicle flies. Crew 13's four people, NASA astronauts Jessica Watkins

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:59.360
and Luke Delaney, Canadian Space Agency astronaut Joshua Kutrick, and Ross Cosmos Cosmonaut

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.400
Sergei Teteriatnikov will stay about six months.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:09.800
The prior U.S. cargo mark was 12 hours and 33 minutes, set by CRS 31 in November 2024.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:15.910
Teams wrapped a static fire campaign on ship 42 and its super heavy booster ahead of flight

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:16.390
15.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:20.470
Tesla Oracle reports, single engine burns came first.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.970
Multi-engine firings and integrated simulations followed.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.730
Company video shows both vehicles still on the pad after each test with no visible damage

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:35.070
on the engines or the structure. Raptor engines on both stages ran full duration burns.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.950
Ground crews said loading and tanking of propellant ran is expected,

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:44.801
and that campaign opens the next test flight window for both stages. Starship orbital

00:01:44.801 --> 00:01:50.561
refueling still sits on an unproven chain of tanker flights, rendezvous timing, and transfer hardware.

00:01:51.141 --> 00:01:55.721
Bloomberg reports, that chain is still required before a crewed lunar mission can proceed.

00:01:55.721 --> 00:02:00.221
Indeed, no public test has shown the full propellant quantity Artemis III would need.

00:02:00.721 --> 00:02:05.281
Engineers are still iterating on seals at the docking interface and on the transfer lines

00:02:05.281 --> 00:02:06.581
inside the payload bay.

00:02:07.141 --> 00:02:12.241
Multiple tanker vehicles must deliver cryogenic propellant, the super cold liquids the engines

00:02:12.241 --> 00:02:14.601
burn, to an orbiting depot stage.

00:02:15.041 --> 00:02:18.861
Exact timing of those meetings remains an open parameter for mission planners.

00:02:19.909 --> 00:02:25.129
SpaceX told regulators it will halt Falcon flights from Cape Canaveral and Bandenberg while

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:29.189
While crews finish Starship Groundwork at Starbase, Gadget Review reported.

00:02:29.829 --> 00:02:32.329
The stand-down lines up with the upcoming test campaign.

00:02:32.829 --> 00:02:35.649
The six-week pause starts now and runs through mid-December.

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:38.969
No Falcon missions sit on the manifest in that window.

00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:42.989
Regulators receive the notice as a routine launch manifest update.

00:02:43.974 --> 00:02:49.054
Stronger orders for satellite and artificial intelligence parts prompted ST microelectronics

00:02:49.054 --> 00:02:50.954
to raise its full-year sales outlook.

00:02:51.494 --> 00:02:52.694
The Wall Street Journal reports.

00:02:52.694 --> 00:02:58.534
The revision covers 2026 revenue. The firm supplies power and sensor parts used

00:02:58.534 --> 00:03:04.294
on multiple SpaceX vehicles and Starlink satellites. Management tied the change directly to rising

00:03:04.294 --> 00:03:08.974
production volumes in both lines. The update came with the quarterly earnings release.

00:03:09.614 --> 00:03:14.094
Satellite demand includes parts for commercial Starlink and for government star shield variants.

00:03:15.101 --> 00:03:21.401
Verizon, AT&amp;T, and T-Mobile asked the Federal Communications Commission, or FCC, to pull

00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:25.361
spectrum out of an upcoming satellite allocation, Tesla Roddy reports.

00:03:26.021 --> 00:03:28.001
The carriers filed ahead of a Thursday vote.

00:03:28.401 --> 00:03:33.181
They want the band kept for Terrestrial 5G instead of being shared with non-geostationary

00:03:33.181 --> 00:03:33.741
systems.

00:03:34.201 --> 00:03:36.861
Satellites that do not hover over one point on Earth.

00:03:37.441 --> 00:03:40.621
Starlink's direct-to-cell service would use portions of that band.

00:03:41.201 --> 00:03:45.581
The request would shrink the proposed satellite allocation by 290 MHz.

00:03:46.485 --> 00:03:51.525
Kennedy Space Center is the historical yardstick NASA Space Flight uses against SpaceX's

00:03:51.525 --> 00:03:57.305
Louisiana launch site plans. Kennedy took five and a half years between ground breaking and first launch.

00:03:57.925 --> 00:04:03.025
Starbase in Texas has run about four and a half years to date. SpaceX has said it intends

00:04:03.025 --> 00:04:08.545
to start launches from Louisiana inside three years after choosing the site. The comparison flags

00:04:08.545 --> 00:04:13.865
differences in permits, the size of the workforce, and how much work they keep in-house. The vehicle

00:04:13.865 --> 00:04:18.625
assembly building needed three years to finish four high-bays size for Saturn class vehicles,

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:23.765
and launch Complex 39A took 22 months to finish the structure before outfitting

00:04:23.765 --> 00:04:24.265
started.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:29.960
Tesla's next-generation roadster showed up inside SpaceX' Hawthorne headquarters two weeks

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.780
after the car was unveiled in public.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:36.020
Tesla Rade reports, it sat next to Starship hardware displays.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:40.800
SpaceX issued no official statement tying the car to a shared engineering program.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.400
The car sat near production tooling used for Falcon and Dragon.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.920
People saw it during ordinary employee and visitor tours.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.701
Elan Musk posted that painting stainless steel adds extra mass and cost the design does

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:56.701
not need, which is why Starship flies unpainted.

